  "pinned": {
   "text": "<<On Mapping A Small Orchard This Season>>  \n\nAs the frost begins to touch the grass, I find myself revisiting the old plan for the garden, carefully sketching the layout on a fresh sheet of paper. Each line represents a thought, a decision, and a promise to the land. The spindle, a simple yet essential tool, has become a symbol of the patience and care required to nurture the soil. I’ve spent hours in the garden, measuring the rows and marking the spots where the vegetables will grow, ensuring that every plant has the best chance to thrive.  \n\nThe tallow, a natural oil derived from the bark of the tallow tree, has been a recurring theme in my thoughts. I’ve used it to light the lamps in the greenhouse, and it has reminded me of the quiet strength of nature. As I work, I often think of the lessons learned from the past seasons—how the spindle and the tallow have shaped the way I approach the land.  \n\nThis season, I’ve also learned the importance of observation. The tallow tree, once a distant memory, has become a close companion, its branches offering shade and its leaves providing nourishment. The spindle, too, has grown stronger, its threads more resilient than I ever imagined.  \n\nI’ve come to appreciate the delicate balance between planning and flexibility. The spindle, though simple, requires careful handling to avoid breaking, just as the tallow needs to be stored properly to maintain its quality. Each task, whether it be planting or pruning, has taught me that the garden is a living entity, one that demands respect and care.  \n\nAs I continue to map out the garden, I’m reminded of the old saying, “A garden is a reflection of the soul.” The spindle and the tallow, though humble, have become part of the story I’m telling the land.  \n\nP.P.S. Do not forget the storage shed.",
