  "pinned_echo": {
   "text": "<<On Mapping A Small Orchard This Season>>  \n\nThis season, mapping the small orchard became a way to connect with the land and the seasons that shaped it. We spent time marking the boundaries, planting new rows, and carefully arranging the tools and materials we would need for the coming months. The sun had begun to warm the soil, and the first signs of growth were emerging, reminding us of the patience and care required to nurture life. We also took time to examine the existing structures, like the old wooden spindles that had stood for years, and thought about how they had once been part of the farm’s daily routine. The tallow from the wild shrubs we collected was not just a resource—it was a reminder of the natural world’s gifts and the importance of respecting them.  \n\nAs we worked, we noticed how the landscape changed with the seasons, and how each element, from the spindles to the tallow, played a role in the orchard’s story. The spindles, though old, still held the weight of history, and the tallow, though humble, was a symbol of the land’s resilience. We made sure to document every detail, from the placement of the spindles to the location of the tallow trees, as these elements would guide us in the future. The orchard was more than just a place to grow fruit—it was a living, breathing part of our community, and we wanted to ensure it remained a place of connection and learning.  \n\nP.P.S. Do not forget the storage shed.",
